  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Description
    
    Seven obsidian chimes suspended in a frame of shadow-steel and ancestor bone of indeterminate age — the bone has gone translucent, its marrow channels visible like frozen rivers. Each bell is hollow, containing captured aurora-light that pulses without heat or flame. The frame is warm to the touch regardless of ambient temperature; no internal source has been identified, only the trapped aurora cycling in rhythm with no discernible heartbeat.
    
    The largest bell bears a deep notch — the tooth-mark of Krathund Gnurl, the orca raider who carried the bells into the sea. Two others show white stress-fractures from The Cav∴lapsum, the catastrophic rupture at Sleek☽ Caemomot that took forty miners and the artifact with them into the deep.
    
    ## Discovery and Provenance
    
    The bells were found at ☽'moth Silvesiris~, a geothermal spring in the Nightshelf's southern reaches, during the early Frozen Peace. The discoverer — an unnamed traveler described in every source as someone "who should not have been there" — carried the instrument from a sealed chamber into the light. No source explains who sealed the chamber or when. The Nightshelf grimoire ☽'☽'numu' Witness contains the inscription most frequently associated with the bells: *what rings between fire and ice cannot be owned, only carried, only borne*.
    
    ## Chain of Custody
    
    The bells have changed hands more often than any artifact of comparable significance. A partial reconstruction:
    
    | **Holder** | **Circumstance of Transfer** |
    |---|---|
    | Unknown traveler | Discovery at ☽'moth Silvesiris~ |
    | The Communion, a Nightshelf cult | Claimed for ritual use |
    | The Cell (owl-sworn), a Nightshelf merchant guild | Stolen for trade |
    | Krathund Gnurl, orca raider | Took the bells into the sea; returned them altered, carrying a depth-resonance the Nightshelf will not name |
    | *(Silent period)* | The blood-working that dispersed Krathund Gnurl scattered his possessions across the Still Pools. The bells fell silent — the only recorded cessation of their vibration |
    | Grandfather Blood, scarred orca exile | Carried the bells long-term, embedded in his body. Entered as evidence of stolen property at a cross-cultural tribunal at the Drowned Ledge, though no bailiff dared remove them. Eventually set them down at ☽'moth Silvesiris~ on volcanic stone above the geothermal spring and walked into the volcanic vents without explanation |
    | Sivruta~, Nightshelf void-mage | Retrieved the bells after navigating corruption thresholds and the judgment of ancient presences in the deep ice |
    
    The bells have been gifted at three Gift-customs — the Nightshelf redistribution rite — each time accompanied by the same warning: *take what calls to you, but remember what answers*. One of these occurred after the tidal catastrophes that emptied the waters around the Berg; the bells' vibration rang through the Aurora Stack's crystal spires with no audience present to hear it.
    
    No one has successfully hidden the bells. No one has successfully destroyed them. When left in place, they anchor themselves to stone through a mechanism the sources describe not as weight but as something requiring understanding before strength.
    
    ## Properties
    
    When struck, the bells produce no audible sound — only a vibration that travels through ice and flesh, a frequency reported to make old wounds ache. Survivors of the Orca Incursion have been observed gripping their weapons involuntarily in the bells' presence. Under certain conditions, this vibration crystallizes into visible geometric lattices that mirror the fractal patterns of ☽'zenith Shard~ Gleam, a wild magic of the deep ice.
    
    The bells activate without bearer intent near geothermal vents and deep-ice anomalies. Their vibration matches frequencies recorded at corruption breach-points — a concordance that orca exile theology interprets as communion with the dead god beneath the sea. Nightshelf acousticians attribute the phenomenon to harmonic properties of volcanic glass interacting with corrupted substrate. Neither explanation accounts for all observations.
    
    Two documented exceptions to the bells' silence deserve note. At Grandfather Blood's tribunal, the bells produced audible sounds resembling ice cracking under nonexistent weight, causing every penguin in the gallery to flinch. And when the void-mage Sivruta~ made contact, the bells resonated with the Mask artifact at Sivruta~'s throat — described as two notes of a chord requiring a third. The aurora-light within the glass shifted through colors that had no names in the Nightshelf tongue.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    The bells are believed to remain in Sivruta~'s custody following retrieval from ☽'moth Silvesiris~. Their resting place before that — the volcanic shelf where Grandfather Blood set them down — held them for an indeterminate period. When found, they were still warm, still pulsing. No one who touched them did so without trembling.
    
    The summary claims the bells are of "impossible age." The deep ice offers no contradiction. I have examined impressions in the Foundation Depths that predate any penguin civilization. The bells' resonance frequencies match some of those impressions. I record this without interpretation.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771584622054_0", anchor: "who should not have been there", text: "Six sources. All anonymous. All using the same formulation. Someone composed this traveler very deliberately, and every subsequent chronicler copied the composition rather than questioning it. Convenient anonymity is not the same as lost provenance.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584622054_1", anchor: "ancestor bone of indeterminate age", text: "The Nightshelf classify 'ancestor bone' as a material category, not a lineage claim — it designates any bone old enough that species identification has become impossible through mineral replacement. Worth stating what the main text politely omits: the bones in that frame may not be penguin. The deep ice contains impressions of things that had bones and were not penguins and were not orcas. I have seen them. I did not enjoy it.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584622054_2", anchor: "took forty miners and the artifact with them into the deep", text: "The Nightshelf memorial at Sleek☽ Caemomot lists thirty-one names. Forty is a rounder number and a better story.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584622054_3", anchor: "set them down at ☽'moth Silvesiris~ on volcanic stone above the geothermal spring and walked into the volcanic vents without explanation", text: "He returned them to where they were found. This is not noted in any orca death-rite I have encountered in the ice. It is, however, structurally identical to a Nightshelf forge-farewell — the completed work set down at the place of its making before the maker withdraws. See my earlier annotation on Grandfather Blood: he spoke fluent penguin, and one wondered what else he learned. I no longer wonder. He learned the forms. He performed them precisely. The Nightshelf sources do not acknowledge this because acknowledging it would mean an orca understood their rites well enough to die inside one.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584622054_4", anchor: "I record this without interpretation", text: "I almost struck this line. It is not true. Recording a resonance match between the bells and pre-penguin impressions in the Foundation Depths and then claiming 'no interpretation' is itself an interpretation — one that says: draw your own conclusion, because mine frightens me. I let it stand because the alternative was writing what I actually think, and this edition will outlast me, and some things should require the next archivist to earn them. If there is a next archivist.", type: :tangent, display: :full
